The '''2004 United States Senate elections''' were held on November 2, 2004, with all [[Classes of United States senators|Class 3 Senate seats]] being contested. They coincided with the [[2004 United States presidential election|re-election]] of [[George W. Bush]] as [[President of the United States|president]] and the [[2004 United States House of Representatives elections|United States House elections]], as well as many state and local elections. Senators who were elected in [[1998 United States Senate elections|1998]], known as [[Classes of United States senators|Senate Class 3]], were seeking re-election or retiring in 2004. 

Republicans won six seats but lost two themselves, giving them a net gain of four seats. Five of the six gains came from states in the region of the [[Southern United States]]. Democrats [[Fritz Hollings]] of [[List of United States senators from South Carolina|South Carolina]], [[John Breaux]] of [[List of United States senators from Louisiana|Louisiana]], [[Bob Graham]] of [[List of United States senators from Florida|Florida]], and [[Zell Miller]] of [[List of United States senators from Georgia|Georgia]] chose to retire, while [[John Edwards]] of [[List of United States senators from North Carolina|North Carolina]] chose to run for the [[2004 Democratic Party presidential primaries|Democratic nomination for president]], and was subsequently selected as the [[2004 Democratic Party vice presidential candidate selection|Democratic nominee for vice president]]. They were succeeded by Republicans [[Jim DeMint]], [[David Vitter]], [[Mel Martinez]], [[Johnny Isakson]], and [[Richard Burr]], respectively.

In [[List of United States senators from South Dakota|South Dakota]], Republican [[John Thune]] defeated the incumbent Senate minority leader [[Tom Daschle]], marking the first and only time since [[1952 United States Senate elections|1952]] that a sitting party leader lost re-election, as well as the only time that person was the minority leader. Republican senator [[Peter Fitzgerald (senator)|Peter Fitzgerald]] of [[List of United States senators from Illinois|Illinois]] chose not to run for re-election, and Democrat (and future president) [[Barack Obama]] won in a landslide, becoming the Senate's only Black member and only the third popularly elected in American history and only the third elected since [[Reconstruction era of the United States|Reconstruction]]. Republican Senator [[Ben Nighthorse Campbell]] of [[List of United States senators from Colorado|Colorado]] chose not to run for re-election, and Democrat [[Ken Salazar]] won the open seat.

This was the third consecutive election cycle for Senate Class 3 in which the Democrats either broke even or lost seats. This also marked the first time since [[1980 United States Senate elections|1980]] in which a presidential candidate from either party won with coattails in the Senate. Additionally, these election results are tied with those of [[1996 United States Senate elections|1996]] and [[1998 United States Senate elections|1998]] as the highest number of Senate seats that the Republicans have held since [[1920 United States Senate elections|1920]]. In contrast, when accounting for their gains in [[2002 United States Senate elections|2002]], it was the last time that the Republicans made net gains in two election cycles in a row as of 2026. Republicans would not make a net gain of seats during a presidential election year again until [[2024 United States Senate elections|2024]]. This is the last time both major parties were on the general election ballot in every race. As of {{CURRENTYEAR}}, this is the most recent election cycle after which the Republican Party ended up with at least 55 Senate seats. 

