Incumbent Republican [[John McCain]] won re-election to a fourth term with his largest victory over Democratic teacher Stuart Starky.<ref>[http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2004/pages/results/states/AZ/S/01/epolls.0.html "Election 2004: U.S. Senate - Arizona - Exit Poll"], [[CNN]]. Retrieved December 23, 2007.</ref>

In 1998, McCain challenged Texas governor [[George W. Bush]] in the presidential primary, and despite winning the New Hampshire primary, he lost the nomination. Solidifying his image as a [[wiktionary:maverick|maverick]], he voted against the [[Bush tax cuts]]. He supported limits on [[stem cell research]]. He had a lopsided favorable rating of 39% to 9% unfavorable in the most recent ''[[The New York Times]]''/[[CBS News]] poll.

Stuart Starky, an eighth-grade teacher in [[South Phoenix]], was widely known as a long-shot challenger. Starky stated, "I truly believe he's going to run for president again."{{clarify|date=September 2026}}<ref>{{cite news |author=Todd S. Purdum |date=August 21, 2004 | title=The 2004 Campaign: the Arizona Senator; Bearhug Politics: Careful Steps to a New Bush-McCain Alliance |newspaper=The New York Times |page=A1 | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2004/08/21/politics/campaign/21mccain.html?th=&pagewanted=all}}</ref> Starky was called by ''[[The Arizona Republic]]'' a "sacrificial lamb"<ref>{{cite news|last1=Norwicki|first1=Dan|last2=Muller|first2=Bill|date=1 March 2007|title=Chapter XII: The 'Maverick' Goes Establishment|publisher=The Arizona Republic|url=https://www.azcentral.com/news/specials/mccain/articles/0301mccainbio-chapter12.html|access-date=20 December 2017|via=archive.azcentral.com|archive-date=24 July 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080724130605/http://www.azcentral.com/news/specials/mccain/articles/0301mccainbio-chapter12.html}}</ref> put on the ballot because there was no chance to beat McCain. During his campaign, he debated McCain twice, once in Tucson and once in Flagstaff. He was also featured on the cover of ''Teacher Magazine'', dubbed the "Unsinkable Stu Starky." Starky was defeated in a [[Landslide victory|landslide]]. However, he had the highest vote per dollar amount in the country, spending only about $15,000 for his campaign (Starky's campaign may have been aided by [[John Kerry]] running for president).{{clarify|date=September 2026}}<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.edweek.org/tm/articles/2004/10/01/02starky.h16.html|title=The Unsinkable Stu Starky - Education Week Teacher|newspaper=Education Week|date=12 October 2004 |access-date=20 December 2017|last1=Cech |first1=Scott J. }}</ref>

