Boxer originally had decided to retire in 2004 but changed her mind to "fight for the right to dissent" against conservatives like Majority Leader [[Tom DeLay]]. Jones was widely considered as the underdog.<ref name="nationalreview.com"/> Jones got a major endorsement from the popular Governor [[Arnold Schwarzenegger]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/news/politicselections/2004-01-15-schwarzenegger-jones_x.htm|title=USATODAY.com - Schwarzenegger to endorse Jones for Senate|website=www.usatoday.com|access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref> The two major candidates had a debate. Pre-election polling had Boxer leading in double digits.<ref>{{cite news|last=Wildermuth|first=John|date=11 August 2004|title=CAMPAIGN 2004 / Jones, Boxer mix it up in only live debate / Challenger plays up Schwarzenegger ties, senator counters|agency=Chronicle Political Writers|url=https://www.sfgate.com/politics/article/CAMPAIGN-2004-Jones-Boxer-mix-it-up-in-only-2734481.php|access-date=20 December 2017|via=articles.sfgate.com}}</ref> Jones did not release a single TV ad during the campaign. Boxer portrayed Jones as too conservative for California, citing his votes in the California Assembly (1982 to 1994) against gun control, increased minimum wage, support for offshore drilling, and a loosening of environmental regulations.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/latimes/access/688264021.html?dids=688264021:688264021&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT&type=current&date=Sep+05,+2004&author=Scott+Martelle&pub=Los+Angeles+Times&desc=The+State;+Bill+Jones%27+Campaign+Quandary;+With+only+tepid+support+from+Bush,+the+GOP+challenger+to+Sen.+Boxer+could+use+a+boost+from+the+governor+--+who+so+far+has+kept+his+distance.&pqatl=google|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130131172255/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/latimes/access/688264021.html?dids=688264021:688264021&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT&type=current&date=Sep+05,+2004&author=Scott+Martelle&pub=Los+Angeles+Times&desc=The+State;+Bill+Jones'+Campaign+Quandary;+With+only+tepid+support+from+Bush,+the+GOP+challenger+to+Sen.+Boxer+could+use+a+boost+from+the+governor+--+who+so+far+has+kept+his+distance.&pqatl=google|archive-date=January 31, 2013|title=Los Angeles Times: Archives - The State|website=pqasb.pqarchiver.com|access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref>

Jones raised about $700,000 more than Boxer during the third quarter, pulling in $2.5 million to Boxer's $1.8 million. Overall, Boxer raised $16 million to Jones' $6.2 million. Boxer spent about $7 million on radio and television ads alone.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/latimes/access/713103661.html?dids=713103661:713103661&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT&type=current&date=Oct+16,+2004&author=Scott+Martelle&pub=Los+Angeles+Times&desc=CALIFORNIA+ELECTIONS;+Jones+Pulls+Out+Cash+He+Lent+to+Campaign&pqatl=google|title=Los Angeles Times: Archives - CALIFORNIA ELECTIONS|website=pqasb.pqarchiver.com|access-date=20 December 2017|archive-date=3 November 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121103184606/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/latimes/access/713103661.html?dids=713103661:713103661&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT&type=current&date=Oct+16,+2004&author=Scott+Martelle&pub=Los+Angeles+Times&desc=CALIFORNIA+ELECTIONS%3B+Jones+Pulls+Out+Cash+He+Lent+to+Campaign&pqatl=google}}</ref>

The election was not close, with Boxer winning by an outsized 20-point margin. Jones only performed well in rural parts of the state. Boxer won almost all major metropolitan areas in the state. The race was called right when the polls closed at 11:00 P.M. EST, and 7:00 P.M. PTZ. Jones conceded defeat to Boxer at 11:12 P.M. EST, and 7:12 PTZ.

