{{See also|List of United States senators from Colorado}}

Incumbent Republican [[Ben Nighthorse Campbell]] decided to retire instead of seeking a third term. Democratic [[Attorney General of Colorado]] [[Ken Salazar]] won the open seat.

Before Campbell's retirement, no prominent Democrat had entered the race, with educator Mike Miles and businessman [[Rutt Bridges]] pursuing the Democratic nomination. After Campbell's retirement, some expected popular Republican governor [[Bill Owens (Colorado politician)|Bill Owens]] to enter the race;<ref>{{Cite web |title=Analysis: Owens focus of Senate talk - UPI Archives |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/2004/03/04/Analysis-Owens-focus-of-Senate-talk/1961078376400/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=UPI |language=en}}</ref> but he declined to run. Campbell's retirement and Owens' decision not to run prompted a number of prominent Democrats to reexamine the race.<ref>{{Cite web |author=Chris Cillizza |date=2004-03-03 |title=Campbell Exit Sparks a Scramble |url=https://rollcall.com/2004/03/03/campbell-exit-sparks-a-scramble/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=Roll Call |language=en-US}}</ref>

On March 10, the same day Owens announced he would not run, U.S. Congressman [[Mark Udall]] entered the race.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Writer |first=Steven K. Paulson/AP |date=2004-03-10 |title=Rep. Udall announces his bid for senate |url=https://www.vaildaily.com/news/rep-udall-announces-his-bid-for-senate/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=www.vaildaily.com |language=en-US}}</ref> The next day, state Attorney General [[Ken Salazar]] entered the race, leading Udall to immediately withdraw and endorse him.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Zaffos |first=Joshua |date=2004-04-12 |title=Colorado Senate race steps into national spotlight |url=http://www.hcn.org/issues/issue-272/colorado-senate-race-steps-into-national-spotlight/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=High Country News |language=en-US}}</ref> Salazar lost to Mike Miles at the state nominating convention. In spite of this loss, the national Democratic Party backed Salazar with contributions from the [[Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee|DSCC]] and promotion of Salazar as the only primary candidate.<ref>{{Cite web |author=Chris Cillizza |date=2004-03-10 |title=Salazar Clears the Field |url=https://rollcall.com/2004/03/10/salazar-clears-the-field/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=Roll Call |language=en-US}}</ref>

