The two candidates got into an ideological battle, as U.S. representative [[Bob Schaffer]] attacked [[Pete Coors]], former CEO and chairman of [[Coors Brewing Company]], because his company had provided benefits to the partners of its [[gay]] and [[lesbian]] employees, in addition to promoting its beer in [[gay bar]]s. Coors defended himself by saying that he was opposed to [[same-sex marriage in the United States|same-sex marriage]], and supported a [[constitutional amendment]] to ban it, although he noted that he supported civil unions for gay couples. According to the ''Rocky Mountain News'', Coors described his company's pro-[[LGBT]] practices as "good business, separate from politics."<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.rockymountainnews.com/drmn/election/article/0,1299,DRMN_36_3034875,00.html|title=Rocky Mountain News: Denver News, Business, Homes, Jobs, Cars, & Information<!-- Bot generated title -->|access-date=20 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040819022154/http://rockymountainnews.com/drmn/election/article/0,1299,DRMN_36_3034875,00.html|archive-date=19 August 2004|df=dmy-all}}</ref>  Coors defeated Schaffer with 61% of the vote in the primary, with a number of analysts citing his high name recognition in the state as a primary factor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Archives |first=L. A. Times |date=2004-08-11 |title=Coors Among Winners in Primaries for U.S. Senate |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2004-aug-11-na-primaries11-story.html |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=Los Angeles Times |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The New York Times > Congressional Quarterly > Washington > Campaign 2004 > CQ Today: Republican Coors Gets Primary Win for Colorado Senate Seat |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/cq/2004/08/13/news-1293530.html |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=archive.nytimes.com}}</ref>

