Pete Coors ran as a moderate conservative. However, Salazar was also a moderate, and a highly popular state attorney general.<ref>{{cite news  |url=http://dir.salon.com/story/opinion/conason/2004/10/22/coors/index.html |title=The Coors campaign's deceptive advertising |access-date=2009-05-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090210110435/http://dir.salon.com/story/opinion/conason/2004/10/22/coors/index.html |archive-date=2009-02-10 }}</ref> Coors is also a great-grandson of [[Adolph Coors]], founder of the brewing company. His father is [[Joseph Coors]], president of the company and founding member of [[The Heritage Foundation]], a conservative think tank. Salazar narrowly won the open seat. It was one of only two Democratic pickups in the 2004 Senate elections; Illinois was the other.

According to the non-partisan [[OpenSecrets]], Coors gave his own campaign $1,213,657 and received individual donations of $60,550 from other Coors family members.{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}}

A state record of over $11 million was raised during the election.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-2762144.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121026101940/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-2762144.html|archive-date=26 October 2012|title=U.S. Senate race rakes in record $11.1 million Candidates ready for tough campaigning in home stretch|date=16 October 2004|via=[[HighBeam Research]]|url-access=subscription|location=Colorado Springs, CO|work=The Gazette|url-status=usurped|access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref>

