In the spring, the campaign hired fundraising staff from the defunct presidential campaigns of [[Howard Dean]] and [[Bob Graham]],<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Shapiro |first=Samantha M. |title=The Dean Machine Marches On |url=https://www.wired.com/2004/09/dean-2/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |magazine=Wired |language=en-US |issn=1059-1028}}</ref> and subsequently posted much higher fundraising numbers over the summer.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Martinez, Castor lead quarterly fundraising |url=https://www.tampabay.com/archive/2004/07/01/martinez-castor-lead-quarterly-fundraising/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=Tampa Bay Times |language=en}}</ref> Online grassroots techniques devised for the Dean campaign (Castor became a [[Dean Dozen]] candidate in August{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}}) were one contributing factor.<ref>{{Cite web |author=VICKIE CHACHERE|agency=Associated Press |title='BettyNet' Web Site Boosts Castor's Senate Bid |url=https://www.theledger.com/story/news/2004/06/14/bettynet-web-site-boosts-castors-senate-bid/26390721007/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=The Ledger |language=en-US}}</ref> Another was the support of [[EMILY's List]], which named Castor as its highest-rated candidate for the 2004 election cycle,{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} even when her support for banning intact dilation and extraction (D&X) abortions was not in line with the EMILY's List support for women's issues.<ref>{{Cite web |title=About Us |url=https://emilyslist.org/about/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=EMILYs List |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Betty Castor on the Issues |url=https://ontheissues.org/Senate/Betty_Castor.htm |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=ontheissues.org}}</ref> The latter was a source of criticism during the August primary heat - a complaint was filed by a Deutsch supporter with the Federal Election Commission accusing inappropriate coordination with EMILY's List.{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} The complaint was dismissed by the Federal Election Commission in 2005.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lauer |first=Nancy Cook |date=2004-08-26 |title=EMILY's List Takes Heat in Florida Primary |url=https://womensenews.org/2004/08/emilys-list-takes-heat-florida-primary/ |access-date=2024-06-01 |website=Women's eNews |language=en-US}}</ref>

Castor's handling of [[Sami Al-Arian]] became another source of criticism during the campaign.{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} In June, [[American Democracy Project (527 group in Florida)|The American Democracy Project]], a [[527 group]] founded by [[Bernie Friedman]],{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} began attacking Castor's handling of the incident, alleging that she had sufficient evidence to fire Al-Arian in the mid-1990s.{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} Castor responded by stating that she never had sufficient evidence to fire Al-Arian, who was a [[tenure]]d professor at the time.{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} On June 29, Senator Graham, who had previously remained outside of the Al-Arian controversy, released a statement saying, "Betty Castor acted appropriately as President of the University of South Florida to deal with Sami Al-Arian".{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} Later, Graham and Senator [[Bill Nelson (politician)|Bill Nelson]] brokered an agreement between the Democratic candidates to refrain from negative campaigning against each other,{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}} although this agreement appeared to break down in the final weeks of the race, when Deutsch launched attack ads on television.{{Citation needed|date=February 2010}}

Despite these controversies, Castor won the Democratic nomination on August 31. She was defeated, however, by Republican candidate [[Mel Martínez]] in a close race on November 2, 2004. The overwhelming support for Martínez among Latinos effectively counterbalanced Castor's relatively high popularity among swing voters throughout the state.

There was some speculation that Castor would run for [[governor of Florida]] in 2006 to replace [[Jeb Bush]], who was ineligible for re-election due to term limits, but she announced in 2005 that she would not be a candidate.

