{{Main|2004 United States Senate election in Illinois}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Illinois}}
Incumbent Republican [[Peter Fitzgerald (senator)|Peter Fitzgerald]] decided to retire after one term. The Democratic and Republican [[Partisan primary|primary election]]s were held in March, which included a total of 15 candidates who combined to spend a record total of over $60 million seeking the [[open seat]].

State Senator and future [[President of the United States|president]] [[Barack Obama]] won the Democratic primary and [[Jack Ryan (politician)|Jack Ryan]] won the Republican primary. Ryan withdrew from the race four days after the ''[[Chicago Tribune]]'' persuaded a California court to release child custody records. The Illinois Republican State Central Committee chose former diplomat [[Alan Keyes]] to replace Ryan as the Republican candidate.

The election was the first for the U.S. Senate in which both major party candidates were [[African American]]. Obama's 43% margin of victory was the largest in the state history of U.S. Senate elections. The inequality in the candidates spending for the fall elections – $14,244,768 by Obama and $2,545,325 by Keyes – is also among the largest in history in both absolute and relative terms.<ref>{{cite book |chapter-url=https://archive.org/details/electio_xxx_2006_00_4209/page/195 |last=Jacobson |first=Gary C. |chapter=The First Congressional Elections After BCRA |title=The Election After Reform: Money, Politics and the Bipartisan Campaign RDUNIAt |publisher=Rowman and Littlefield |isbn=978-0-7425-3870-2 |page=[https://archive.org/details/electio_xxx_2006_00_4209/page/195 195] |date=March 30, 2006 |access-date=October 27, 2012 }}</ref>

[[File:20040814 Bud Billiken Obama float.JPG|thumb|Obama-for-Senate float at the 2004 [[Bud Billiken Parade and Picnic]]]]

Fitzgerald's predecessor, Democrat [[Carol Moseley Braun]], declined to run. Obama, a member of the [[Illinois Senate]] since 1997 and an unsuccessful [[2000 Illinois's 1st congressional district election|2000 Democratic primary]] challenger to four-term incumbent U.S. Rep. [[Bobby Rush]] for Rush's [[Illinois's 1st congressional district|U.S House]] seat, launched a campaign committee at the beginning of July 2002 to run for the U.S. Senate, 21 months before the March 2004 primary.<ref>{{Cite news |author=Neal, Steve |date=July 3, 2002 |title=Obama could add drama to Senate race |work=Chicago Sun-Times |page=41 |format=paid archive |url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=CSTB&p_theme=cstb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&s_dispstring=(Obama)%20AND%20date(7/3/2002%20to%207/3/2002)&p_field_date-0=YMD_date&p_params_date-0=date:B,E&p_text_date-0=7/3/2002%20to%207/3/2002)&p_field_advanced-0=&p_text_advanced-0=(Obama)&xcal_numdocs=20&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&xcal_useweights=no |access-date=November 4, 2008}}</ref> Two months later, he had [[David Axelrod]] lined up to do his campaign media.<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 1, 2002 |title=No shortage of candidates for U.S. Senate run in '04 |work=The State Journal-Register |page=15 |format=paid archive |url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=JR&p_theme=jr&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_text_search-0=Obama%20AND%20Axelrod&s_dispstring=Obama%20Axelrod%20AND%20date(9/1/2002%20to%209/1/2002)&p_field_date-0=YMD_date&p_params_date-0=date:B,E&p_text_date-0=9/1/2002%20to%209/1/2002)&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&xcal_useweights=no |access-date=November 4, 2008}}</ref> Obama formally announced his candidacy on January 21, 2003,<ref>{{Cite news |author1=Pearson, Rick |author2=Chase, John |date=January 22, 2003 |title=Legislator in race to unseat Fitzgerald; Democrat seeks 2004 nomination for U.S. Senate |work=Chicago Tribune |page=4 (Metro) |format=paid archive |url=https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/279331161.html?dids=279331161:279331161&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT |access-date=November 4, 2008 |archive-date=October 24, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121024141029/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/279331161.html?dids=279331161:279331161&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT }}</ref> four days after former U.S. Sen. [[Carol Moseley Braun]] announced she would not seek a rematch with U.S. Sen. [[Peter Fitzgerald (politician)|Peter Fitzgerald]].<ref>{{Cite news |author=Krol, Eric |date=January 18, 2003 |title=Ex-senator doesn't want rematch with Fitzgerald |work=Daily Herald (Arlington Heights) |page=11 |format=paid archive |url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=ADHB&p_theme=adhb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_text_search-0=Ex-senator&s_dispstring=Ex-senator%20AND%20date(1/18/2003%20to%201/18/2003)&p_field_date-0=YMD_date&p_params_date-0=date:B,E&p_text_date-0=1/18/2003%20to%201/18/2003)&xcal_numdocs=20&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&xcal_useweights=no |access-date=November 4, 2008 }}<br />{{Cite news |author=Zorn, Eric |date=January 18, 2003 |title=Moseley-Braun gives Democrats reason for hope |work=Chicago Tribune |page=15 (Metro) |format=paid archive |url=https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/278226871.html?dids=278226871:278226871&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT |access-date=November 4, 2008 |archive-date=November 3, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121103000346/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/278226871.html?dids=278226871:278226871&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT }}</ref>

On April 15, 2003, with six Democrats already running and three Republicans threatening to run against him,<ref>{{Cite news |author=Krol, Eric |date=December 9, 2002 |title= Why senator ending up more isolated; Fitzgerald's style has both Democrats and Republicans planning to challenge him |work=Daily Herald (Arlington Heights) |page=1 |format=paid archive |url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=ADHB&p_theme=adhb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_text_search-0=Why%20AND%20senator%20AND%20ending%20AND%20up%20AND%20more%20AND%20isolated&s_dispstring=Why%20senator%20ending%20up%20more%20isolated%20AND%20date(12/9/2002%20to%2012/9/2002)&p_field_date-0=YMD_date&p_params_date-0=date:B,E&p_text_date-0=12/9/2002%20to%2012/9/2002)&xcal_numdocs=20&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&xcal_useweights=no |access-date=November 4, 2008}}<br />{{Cite news |author=Neal, Steve |date=April 16, 2003 |title=Writing was on the wall after latest Fitzgerald polls |work=Chicago Sun-Times |page=55 |format=paid archive |url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=CSTB&p_theme=cstb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&s_dispstring=(Writing%20was%20on%20the%20wall)%20AND%20date(4/16/2003%20to%204/16/2003)&p_field_date-0=YMD_date&p_params_date-0=date:B,E&p_text_date-0=4/16/2003%20to%204/16/2003)&p_field_advanced-0=&p_text_advanced-0=(Writing%20was%20on%20the%20wall)&xcal_numdocs=20&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&xcal_useweights=no |access-date=November 4, 2008}}</ref> incumbent Fitzgerald announced he would not seek a second term in 2004,<ref>{{Cite news |author=Wilgoren, Jodi |date=April 16, 2003 |title=Illinois Senator announces he won't seek re-election |work=The New York Times |page=A.10 |url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9803E6D81E3BF935A25757C0A9659C8B63&sec=&spon=&pagewanted=all |access-date=November 4, 2008}}</ref> and three weeks later popular Republican former governor [[Jim Edgar]] declined to run,<ref>{{Cite news |author1=Pearson, Rick |author2=Chase, John |date=May 10, 2003 |title=GOP Senate floodgates open as Edgar says no |work=Chicago Tribune |page=1 |format=paid archive |url=https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/334607871.html?dids=334607871:334607871&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT |access-date=November 4, 2008 |archive-date=November 3, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121103000356/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/334607871.html?dids=334607871:334607871&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT }}</ref> leading to wide open Democratic and Republican primary races with 15 candidates, including seven millionaires<ref>{{Cite news |author=Davey, Monica |date=March 7, 2004 |title=Closely watched Illinois Senate race attracts 7 candidates in millionaire range |work=The New York Times |page=1.19 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2004/03/07/politics/campaign/07ILLI.html?ei=5007&en=45e065f19b4f8d4f&ex=1393995600&partner=USERLAND&pagewanted=all&position= |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130130121323/http://www.nytimes.com/2004/03/07/politics/campaign/07ILLI.html?ei=5007&en=45e065f19b4f8d4f&ex=1393995600&partner=USERLAND&pagewanted=all&position= |archive-date=January 30, 2013 |access-date=November 4, 2008}} <br />{{Cite news |date=March 7, 2004 |title=Candidate wealth; net worth of the richest Illinois candidates for the U.S. Senate |work=The New York Times |page=1.19 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/imagepages/2004/03/07/national/20040307_ILLI_GRAPH.html |access-date=November 4, 2008}}</ref> (triggering the first application of the Millionaires' Amendment of the 2002 [[Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act|McCain–Feingold Act]]), in the most expensive Senate primary in U.S. history.<ref>{{Cite news |author=Justice, Glen |date=October 17, 2003 |title=In races with one deep pocket, the law tries to tailor a second |work=The New York Times |page=A.1 |url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=950DE3D7143EF934A25753C1A9659C8B63&sec=&spon=&pagewanted=all |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130130103118/http://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=950DE3D7143EF934A25753C1A9659C8B63&sec=&spon=&pagewanted=all |archive-date=January 30, 2013 |access-date=November 4, 2008}}</ref>

Obama touted his legislative experience and early public opposition to the [[Iraq War]] to distinguish himself from his Democratic primary rivals. Illinois Comptroller [[Daniel Hynes|Dan Hynes]] won the endorsement of the [[AFL–CIO]]. Obama obtained the support of three of the state's largest and most active member unions: [[American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees|AFSCME]], [[Service Employees International Union|SEIU]], and the [[American Federation of Teachers|Illinois Federation of Teachers]]. Hynes and multimillionaire former securities trader [[Blair Hull]] each won the endorsements of two of the nine Democratic Illinois members of the US House of Representatives. Obama had the endorsements of four: [[Jesse Jackson, Jr.]], [[Danny K. Davis|Danny Davis]], [[Lane Evans]], and [[Jan Schakowsky]].

Obama surged into the lead after he finally began television advertising in Chicago in the final three weeks of the campaign, which was expanded to [[downstate Illinois]] during the last six days of the campaign. The ads included strong endorsements by the five largest newspapers in Illinois—the ''[[Chicago Tribune]]'', ''[[Chicago Sun-Times]]'', ''[[Daily Herald (Arlington Heights)|Daily Herald]]'', ''[[The Rockford Register Star]]'', and ''[[Peoria Journal Star]]''—and a testimonial by [[Sheila Simon]] that Obama was "cut from that same cloth" as her father, the late former U.S. senator [[Paul Simon (politician)|Paul Simon]], who had planned to endorse and campaign for Obama before his unexpected death in December 2003.
<ref>{{Cite news |author=Rosenbaum, David E. |date=December 10, 2003 |title=Paul Simon, former Senator from Illinois, is dead at 75 |newspaper=The New York Times |page=A29
