As a result of the GOP and Democratic primaries, Democrat [[Barack Obama]] was pitted against Republican [[Jack Ryan (Senate candidate)|Jack Ryan]].

Ryan trailed Obama in early polls, after the media reported that Ryan had assigned Justin Warfel, a Ryan campaign worker, to track Obama's appearances.<ref name="Mendell">{{Cite news |first=David |last=Mendell |title=Ryan aide to give Obama more space |url=http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/specials/elections/chi-0405230433may23,1,3572265.story |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |date=May 23, 2004 |access-date=February 11, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071205204729/http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/specials/elections/chi-0405230433may23,1,3572265.story |archive-date=December 5, 2007 |df=mdy}}</ref> The tactic backfired when multiple people, including Ryan's supporters, criticized this activity. Ryan's spokesman apologized, and promised that Warfel would give Obama more space. Obama acknowledged that it is standard practice to film an opponent in public, and Obama said he was satisfied with Ryan's decision to have Warfel back off.<ref name="Mendell" />

As the campaign progressed, the lawsuit brought by the ''[[Chicago Tribune]]'' to open [[Child custody laws in the United States|child custody]] files from Ryan's divorce was still continuing. Obama's backers emailed reporters about the divorce controversy, but refrained from on-the-record commentary.<ref name="Fornek"/> On March 29, 2004, Los Angeles Superior Court Judge Robert Schnider ruled that several of the Ryans' divorce records should be opened to the public, and ruled that a court-appointed referee would later decide which custody files should remain sealed to protect the interests of Ryan's young child.<ref>Ford, Liam. [http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/specials/elections/chi-0403300182mar30,1,7640086.story "Some Ryan divorce files should be unsealed"], ''Chicago Tribune'' (March 30, 2004).</ref> A few days later, on April 2, 2004, Obama changed his position about the Ryans' soon-to-be-released divorce records, and called on Democrats to not inject them into the campaign.<ref name="Fornek">Fornek, Scott. [http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=CSTB&p_theme=cstb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&s_dispstring=(Obama:%20Back%20off%20divorce%20files)%20AND%20date(4/3/2004%20to%204/3/2004)&p_field_date-0=YMD_date&p_params_date-0=date:B,E&p_text_date-0=4/3/2004%20to%204/3/2004)&p_field_advanced-0=&p_text_advanced-0=(Obama:%20Back%20off%20divorce%20files)&xcal_numdocs=20&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&xcal_useweights=no "Obama: Back off divorce files"], ''Chicago Sun-Times''  (April 3, 2004).</ref>

On June 22, 2004, after receiving the report from the court-appointed referee, the judge released the files that were deemed consistent with the interests of the Ryans' child. In those files, [[Jeri Ryan]] alleged that Jack Ryan had taken her to sex clubs in several cities, intending for them to have sex in public.<ref name="nyt1">{{Cite news |first=Stephen |last=Kinzer |author-link=Stephen Kinzer |author2=Jo Napolitano  |title=Illinois Senate Campaign Thrown Into Prurient Turmoil |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2004/06/23/us/illinois-senate-campaign-thrown-into-prurient-turmoil.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 23, 2004 |page=A14 |access-date=February 10, 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.signonsandiego.com/news/politics/20040622-0304-illinoissenate.html |title=Illinois Republican vows to stay in Senate race despite embarrassing allegations  |access-date=August 12, 2007 |date=June 22, 2004 |author=Lannan, Maura Kelly |work=The San Diego Union-Tribune |agency=Associated Press}}</ref>

The decision to release the files generated much controversy because it went against both parents' direct request, and because it reversed the earlier decision to seal the papers in the best interest of the child. Jim Oberweis, Ryan's defeated GOP opponent, commented, "these are allegations made in a divorce hearing, and we all know people tend to say things that aren't necessarily true in divorce proceedings when there is money involved and custody of children involved."<ref name="nyt1" />

Although their [[sensationalism|sensational nature]] made the revelations fodder for [[Tabloid (newspaper format)|tabloid]] and television programs specializing in such stories, the files were also newsworthy because of questions about whether Ryan had accurately described the documents to GOP party leaders. Prior to release of the documents, Ryan had told leading Republicans that five percent of the divorce file could cause problems for his campaign.<ref>Martinez, Michael; Pearson, Rick. [http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/local/chi-0406180364jun18,1,3423709.story "Court sets release of Ryan's divorce file"], ''Chicago Tribune'' (June 18, 2004).</ref> After the documents were released, GOP officials, including state GOP chair [[Judy Baar Topinka]], said they felt Ryan had misleadingly indicated that the records would not be embarrassing.<ref>Pearson, Rick; Ford, Liam. [http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/local/chi-0406230356jun23,1,998870.story?coll=chi-homepagepromo451-fea "GOP leaders say they felt misled on Ryan file"], ''Chicago Tribune'' (June 23, 2004).</ref>

That charge of dishonesty led to intensifying calls for Ryan's withdrawal, though Topinka, who was considering running herself, said after the June 25 withdrawal that Ryan's "decision was a personal one" and that the state GOP had not pressured Ryan to drop out.<ref>Ford, Liam; Bush, Rudolph. [http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/local/chi-0406260116jun26,1,277974.story?coll=chi-news-hed "Ryan Quits Race"], ''Chicago Tribune'' June 26, 2004.</ref> Ryan's campaign ended less than a week after the custody records were opened, and he officially filed the documentation to withdraw on July 29, 2004. Obama was left without an opponent.

The Illinois Republican State Central Committee chose former diplomat [[Alan Keyes]] to replace Ryan as the Republican candidate. Keyes, a [[American conservatism|conservative Republican]] from Maryland, faced difficulties. He had few ties to Illinois political leaders. The lack of an opponent allowed Obama to campaign throughout the more conservative downstate regions to build up name recognition. Keyes was seen as a [[carpetbagger]], only establishing legal residency in [[Calumet City, Illinois]] days before running. A ''[[Chicago Tribune]]'' [[editorial]] stated, "Mr. Keyes may have noticed a large body of water as he flew into [[O'Hare International Airport|O'Hare]]. That is called [[Lake Michigan]]."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/2004/08/06/the-gops-rent-a-senator/ |title=Commentary: The GOP's rent-a-senator  |access-date=January 21, 2013 |date=August 6, 2004 |work=Chicago Tribune}}</ref> In 2000, Keyes had attacked [[Hillary Clinton]] for running for US senator from New York even though she had never lived there, calling her a carpetbagger.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2004/08/10/opinion/10tue2.html |title=Plan B for Illinois |date= August 10, 2004 |work= The New York Times |access-date= December 25, 2009 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20111228091458/http://www.nytimes.com/2004/08/10/opinion/10tue2.html |archive-date=December 28, 2011}}</ref> Keyes attacked Barack Obama for voting against a bill that would have outlawed a form of late-term abortion.<ref>{{cite news |last=Robinson |first=Mike |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna5654128 |title=Keyes assails Obama's abortion views |publisher=NBC News |agency=Associated Press |access-date=December 27, 2009 |date=August 9, 2004}}</ref>

Obama ran the most successful Senate campaign in 2004, and was so far ahead in polls that he soon began to campaign outside of Illinois in support of other Democratic candidates. He gave large sums of campaign funds to other candidates and the [[Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee]], and sent a number of his volunteers to work on other races, including that of eventual three-term Congresswoman [[Melissa Bean]], who defeated then-Congressman [[Phil Crane]] in that year's election. Obama and Keyes differed on a number of issues, including [[education voucher|school vouchers]] and tax cuts, both of which Keyes supported and Obama opposed.<ref>{{Cite web|date=|title=Alan Keyes Archives: Debates|url=http://www.keyesarchives.com/debates.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070225055002/http://www.keyesarchives.com/debates.htm|archive-date=2007-02-25|website=keyesarchives.com}}</ref>

