Incumbent Democrat [[Evan Bayh]] won re-election to a second term, beating Republican [[Marvin Scott]], a professor at [[Butler University]].

In September, Bayh had $6.5 million cash on hand. Scott's strategy of trying to paint Bayh as too liberal failed to gain traction. Bayh was viewed early in 2004 as a serious vice presidential candidate for [[John Kerry]]. Bayh was on the final shortlist for a Kerry running mate, but North Carolina senator [[John Edwards]] was chosen instead.

