During his re-election bid in 2004, controversy erupted when Bunning described Mongiardo as looking "like one of Saddam Hussein's sons." Bunning apologized, then later went on to declare that Mongiardo's "thugs" had assaulted his wife.<ref>{{cite news | title=Bunning apologizes for Saddam comparison | url=https://www.usatoday.com/news/politicselections/state/kentucky/2004-04-01-bunning-apology_x.htm | work=[[USA Today]] | agency=Associated Press | date=April 1, 2004 | access-date=March 5, 2008}}</ref><ref name=Farhi>{{Cite news | author=Paul Farhi | title=Bunning's Wild Pitches Tighten Kentucky Senate Race | url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A34265-2004Oct15.html | newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] | date=October 15, 2004 | access-date=March 5, 2008 | pages=A06}}</ref>

Bunning had an estimated $4 million campaign war chest, while Mongiardo had only $600,000. The Democrats began increasing financial support to Mongiardo when it became apparent that Bunning's bizarre behavior was costing him votes, purchasing more than $800,000 worth of additional television airtime on his behalf.

The November 2 election was one of the closest in Kentucky history. Mongiardo was initially leading, with as many as 80% of the returns coming in. However, Bunning eventually won by just over one percentage point. Some analysts felt that because of President George Bush's 20% margin of victory in the state, Bunning was able to effectively ride the president's coattails to victory.

