Carson and Coburn engaged each other head-on in one of the year's most brutal Senate contests. Coburn and the [[National Republican Senatorial Committee]] attacked Carson for being too liberal for Oklahoma, and for being a vote in lockstep with [[John Kerry]], [[Hillary Clinton]], and [[Ted Kennedy]]. To drive the point home, one Coburn television ad accused Carson of being "dangerously liberal" and not supporting the [[war on terrorism]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X_zHqYD2KAU |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/X_zHqYD2KAU |archive-date=2021-12-21 |url-status=live|title=Tom Coburn 2004 U.S. Senate "Dangerously Liberal"|last=tulsaschoolboard|date=25 February 2012|access-date=20 December 2017|via=YouTube}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Coburn was aided in this effort by the fact that the [[John Kerry presidential campaign, 2004|Kerry campaign]] did not contest the state of Oklahoma and that incumbent president [[George W. Bush]] was expected to win Oklahoma comfortably. This was compounded by the fact that [[Vice-President of the United States|Vice-President]] [[Dick Cheney]] campaigned for Coburn and appeared in several television advertisements for him.<ref>http://www3.nationaljournal.com/members/adspotlight/2004/10/1007oksen1.htm{{Dead link|date=August 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Carson countered by emphasizing his [[Stilwell, Oklahoma|Stilwell]] roots<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eWeBg0-nST4 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/eWeBg0-nST4 |archive-date=2021-12-21 |url-status=live|title=Brad Carson U.S. Senate 2004 "Stilwell"|last=tulsaschoolboard|date=25 February 2012|access-date=20 December 2017|via=YouTube}}{{cbignore}}</ref> and his moderation, specifically, bringing attention to the fact that he fought for greater governmental oversight of nursing home care for the elderly.<ref>{{cite news|date=22 April 2001|title=Carson's First Stand Should Feds Examine Nursing Homes?|work=newsok.com|url=http://newsok.com/carsons-first-stand-should-feds-examine-nursing-homes/article/2738499|access-date=20 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151208102803/http://newsok.com/carsons-first-stand-should-feds-examine-nursing-homes/article/2738499|archive-date=2015-12-08}}</ref> Carson responded to the attacks against him by countering that his opponent had committed [[Medicaid]] fraud years prior, in an event that reportedly left a woman sterilized without her consent.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A21673-2004Sep14.html|title=Okla. Senate Candidate Is Accused of Fraud (washingtonpost.com)|website=www.washingtonpost.com|access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref> Ultimately, however, Carson was not able to overcome [[Oklahoma]]'s conservative nature and Senator [[John Kerry|Kerry]]'s [[2004 United States presidential election in Oklahoma|abysmal performance in Oklahoma]], and he was defeated by Coburn by 11.5%. As of 2022, the result remains the closest the Democrats have come to winning a Senate election in Oklahoma since Republican [[Don Nickles]] was first elected to the Senate by 8.7% [[1980 United States Senate election in Oklahoma|in 1980]].

