{{Main|2004 United States Senate election in Wisconsin}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Wisconsin}}
Incumbent Democrat [[Russ Feingold]] won re-election to a third term.

Republican businessman and Army veteran Tim Michels insisted he had more real world experience than Feingold, someone he called an "extreme liberal" who was out of touch with Wisconsin voters.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-112626866.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121104080030/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-112626866.html|archive-date=4 November 2012|title=Michels Touts Rural Roots in Joining Gop Senate Race|date=27 January 2004|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=The Capital Times|url-status=usurped|access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref> Feingold countered by saying that any Republican would be a rubber stamp for President Bush. The incumbent had $2.2 million in the bank, while Michels had already spent $1 million in the primary and had only about $150,000 left.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_6caAAAAIBAJ&pg=6652,3445599&dq=tim+michels&hl=en|title=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel - Google News Archive Search|website=news.google.com|access-date=20 December 2017}}{{Dead link|date=June 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

When the [[National Republican Senatorial Committee|NRSC]] was finally convinced in October that Michels had a shot, they pledged $600,000 for him.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=PD&s_site=twincities&p_multi=SP&p_theme=realcities&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_topdoc=1&p_text_direct-0=1061D02F1E0B4413&p_field_direct-0=document_id&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&s_trackval=GooglePM|title=Pioneer Press: Search Results|website=nl.newsbank.com|access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref>

On October 1, a poll showed Feingold leading 52% to 39%.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Zielinski|first=Graeme|date=1 October 2004|title=Feingold holds big lead over Michels, poll says|url=http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=MWSB&p_theme=mwsb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_topdoc=1&p_text_direct-0=1057665A96950BA6&p_field_direct-0=document_id&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&s_trackval=GooglePM|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110609093231/http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=MWSB&p_theme=mwsb&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_topdoc=1&p_text_direct-0=1057665A96950BA6&p_field_direct-0=document_id&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&s_trackval=GooglePM|archive-date=2011-06-09|access-date=2021-07-10|website=nl.newsbank.com|publisher=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel}}</ref> In mid-October, another poll showed Feingold winning 48% to 43%. A poll at the end of the month showed him leading 51% to 36%.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=PKgaAAAAIBAJ&pg=6021,4541874&dq=tim+michels+poll&hl=en|title=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel - Google News Archive Search|website=news.google.com|access-date=20 December 2017}}{{Dead link|date=June 2022 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

